== Problem 2: where to meet
OK   random graphs: two Dijkstra runs + scan == exhaustive simple-path search (value and the set of best cities); undirected, and directed (one-way roads) in a quarter of the cases  [4000 instances, 513 with no common reachable city]
OK   trap: minimizing d(p,t) + d(q,t) can pick a city with a later meeting  [694 random instances]
OK   trap: the best city can lie off every shortest p-q path  [72 random instances]
OK   undirected: every meeting time is >= d(p,q)/2
OK   trap example: meeting times p 10, a 9, q 10, c 6; best city c; d(p,q) = 10 along p a q, c is on no shortest p-q path (6 + 6 = 12 > 10)  [{'p': 10, 'a': 9, 'q': 10, 'c': 6}]
OK   sum trap (Ex 8.14's example): on p - a - q with two roads of length 1 the sum is 2 everywhere, but only a gives meeting time 1
OK   mid-road remark: one road p-q of length 2: best city time 2, halfway point time 1
== Problem 3: looped trees
OK   random looped trees, n = 1..12: the split-root dag + two DagSSSP runs == Floyd-Warshall for every pair  [81979 pairs: {'desc': 19005, 'notdesc': 53219, 'same': 9755, 'to_root': 8255, 'from_root': 8255}]
OK   random looped trees: closed form D(v) - D(u) (u ancestor of v) / L(u) + D(v) == Floyd-Warshall
OK   random looped trees: the one-query procedure (walk up from v, postorder of the subtree of u) == Floyd-Warshall
OK   leaves <= (n+1)/2 and E = n - 1 + leaves <= (3n-1)/2
OK   O(1) queries after O(n) preprocessing (D, L, and 'u = v or u is an ancestor of v' as pre(u) <= pre(v) and post(u) >= post(v)) == Floyd-Warshall
OK   H is a dag: preorder (r_out first) then r_in is a topological order; H has as many edges as G
OK   a looped tree is strongly connected (every distance finite)
OK   a full binary tree attains E = (3n-1)/2 (n = 7: 10 edges)
OK   one leaf: the looped tree is a single cycle, and the formula is right with any vertex as the root
OK   given only the graph: with >= 2 leaves the root is the unique vertex of in-degree != 1 (in-degree = number of leaves); with one leaf every in-degree is 1
OK   handout tree: D = r 0, a 5, b 8, c 22, d 5, e 45, f 9, g 18, h 23  [{'r': 0, 'a': 5, 'b': 8, 'f': 9, 'g': 18, 'h': 23, 'c': 22, 'd': 5, 'e': 45}]
OK   handout tree: L = r 21, a 16, b 17, c 27, d 16, e 4, f 16, g 7, h 42  [{'e': 4, 'c': 27, 'd': 16, 'a': 16, 'g': 7, 'h': 42, 'f': 16, 'b': 17, 'r': 21}]
OK   handout tree: delta(b, d) = L(b) + D(d) = 17 + 5 = 22 along b f g r a d (1 + 9 + 7 + 5 + 0)
OK   handout tree: the other way out of b, through h, costs 1 + 14 + 42 = 57 to the root (62 to d)
OK   handout tree: no way up -- delta(c, d) = L(c) + D(d) = 27 + 5 = 32 along c e r a d, not 17 + 0 through a
OK   handout tree: Exercise 8.1's numbers delta(a,h) = 39, delta(c,a) = 32, delta(a,e) = 40 (tree path; via the root 16 + 45 = 61)
OK   handout tree: delta(r, v) = D(v) and delta(u, r) = L(u) for all u != r
OK   handout tree: n = 9, 4 leaves, 12 edges <= (3*9-1)/2 = 13
OK   handout tree: closed form right for all 81 pairs
OK   handout tree: the case u = v must go to D(v) - D(u) = 0 -- L(u) + D(u) > 0 for every u (strict 'ancestor' would be wrong on the diagonal)
